  "answer_text": "Hipparchus of Nicaea (c. 190 \u2013 c. 120 BC) was an ancient Greek astronomer, mathematician, and geographer. He is widely regarded as one of the greatest scientific minds of antiquity. While many of his original writings have been lost, historical records (most notably from Ptolemy\u2019s *Almagest*) credit him with inventing several revolutionary mathematical systems, instruments, and scientific scales.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what Hipparchus invented, developed, or is highly reputed to have created:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Major Mathematical & Scientific Inventions\n\n*   **Trigonometry (The Table of Chords):** Hipparchus is widely considered the **inventor of trigonometry**. To solve complex astronomical problems, he developed a systematic way to calculate the relationship between angles and the lengths of chords in a circle. He compiled the first-known **table of chords**, which was the ancient precursor to modern trigonometric tables (specifically the sine function).\n*   **The Stellar Magnitude Scale:** Hipparchus invented the first system to classify stars by their brightness. He divided the stars visible to the naked eye into six categories: the brightest stars were designated as \"first magnitude,\" while the faintest visible stars were \"sixth magnitude\". This basic system, mathematically refined in the 19th century, is still used by astronomers today. \n*   **A Systematic Geographic Coordinate Grid:** While concepts of latitude and longitude existed before him, Hipparchus was the first to invent a systematic method of using astronomical observations (rather than just estimated travel distances) to determine the latitude and longitude of specific places. He proposed a global grid system to accurately map the Earth.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Reputed Astronomical Instruments\n\nHistorians and ancient texts credit Hipparchus with inventing or significantly improving several physical instruments used to observe the heavens:\n\n*   **The Planispheric Astrolabe (Possible Inventor):** Many historians of astronomy believe Hipparchus was the inventor of the planispheric astrolabe. This device\u2014often called an ancient \"analog computer\"\u2014projected the three-dimensional celestial sphere onto a flat plane, allowing astronomers to calculate the positions of the sun and stars, tell the time, and determine latitude.\n*   **The Armillary Sphere:** Hipparchus is often credited with either inventing or vastly improving the armillary sphere\u2014a model of objects in the sky consisting of a spherical framework of rings. He likely used this device to help map and measure the coordinates of stars.\n*   **An Improved Dioptra (or Classical Theodolite):** Ptolemy credits Hipparchus with inventing or heavily modifying a dioptra (a sighting tube or surveying instrument) to measure the apparent diameters of the Sun and Moon and precisely calculate angles in the sky.\n\n---\n\n### 3. His Most Famous Discoveries (Not Inventions)\n\nWhile not \"inventions\" in the physical sense, no summary of Hipparchus is complete without his monumental discoveries, which laid the foundation for modern astronomy:\n\n*   **The Precession of the Equinoxes:** Hipparchus discovered that the Earth\u2019s rotational axis slowly wobbles over time (a cycle that takes about 26,000 years). By comparing his star observations with records from 150 years earlier, he noticed the positions of stars had slightly shifted.\n*   **The First Comprehensive Western Star Catalog:** He compiled the first detailed, systematic Western star catalog, mapping the positions and brightness of approximately 850 stars.\n*   **Precision Solar and Lunar Measurements:** He calculated the length of the tropical year to within 6.5 minutes of its actual length. Using astronomical parallax during a solar eclipse, he also calculated the distance from the Earth to the Moon to be between 59 and 67 Earth radii (the actual average is roughly 60 Earth radii)\u2014an astonishingly accurate feat for his time.",
